The surface charge density of a closed surface area having charge $\mathrm{Q}$ is given by

\sigma=\frac{\text { Charge }}{\text { Area }}=\frac{\mathrm{Q}}{\mathrm{A}} \text { or } \mathrm{Q}=\sigma \mathrm{A}

Thus, the charges on sphere P and Q having same charge density as shown in the figure below is given by

$<img src="/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fneet%252F8OrFtZ-L1DuMNZLMCDNi5eNJAxqJkaQSPbtHl0psGHA.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2" data-qx-orig-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fneet%2F8OrFtZ-L1DuMNZLMCDNi5eNJAxqJkaQSPbtHl0psGHA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media" data-qx-storage-src="https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fneet%2F8OrFtZ-L1DuMNZLMCDNi5eNJAxqJkaQSPbtHl0psGHA.original.fullsize.png?alt=media">$ \\ 

\begin{gathered}
\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{P}}=\sigma \times 4 \pi \mathrm{R}^2=4 \pi \sigma \mathrm{R}^2 \\
\text { and } \mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{Q}}=\sigma \times 4 \pi(2 \mathrm{R})^2=16 \pi \sigma \mathrm{R}^2
\end{gathered}

when they are brought in contact with each other, the total charge will be

\begin{aligned}
\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{t}} & =\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{P}}+\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{Q}} \\
= & 4 \pi \sigma \mathrm{R}^2+16 \pi \sigma \mathrm{R}^2 \text { [From Eq. (i) and (ii)] } \\
& =20 \pi \sigma \mathrm{R}^2
\end{aligned}

In connection of two charged conducting bodies, the potential will become same on both, i.e.

\begin{aligned}
\frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{P}}}{4 \pi \in_0 \mathrm{R}}=\frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{Q}}}{4 \pi \epsilon_0 2 \mathrm{R}} \\
\Rightarrow \quad \frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{P}}}{\mathrm{R}}=\frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{Q}}}{2 \mathrm{R}} \Rightarrow \frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{P}}}{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{Q}}}=\frac{1}{2}
\end{aligned}

So, the charges on the sphere $\mathrm{P}$ and $\mathrm{Q}$ after separation will be distributed as

\Rightarrow \quad Q_P^{\prime}=\frac{1}{3} Q_{\mathrm{t}} \text { and } \mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{\prime}=\frac{2}{3} \mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{t}}

After separation, the new surface charge densities on $\mathrm{P}$ and $\mathrm{Q}$ will be

\begin{aligned}
\sigma_{\mathrm{P}}=\frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{P}}^{\prime}}{\text { Area }} & =\frac{1}{3} \frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{t}}}{\text { Area }}=\frac{1}{3} \frac{20 \pi \sigma \mathrm{R}^2}{4 \pi \mathrm{R}^2}=\frac{5}{3} \sigma \\
\text { and } \sigma_{\mathrm{Q}}=\frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{Q}}^{\prime}}{\text { Area }} & =\frac{2}{3} \frac{\mathrm{Q}_{\mathrm{t}}}{\text { Area }}=\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{20 \pi \sigma \mathrm{R}^2}{4 \pi(2 \mathrm{R})^2} \\
& =\frac{2}{3} \times \frac{5}{4} \sigma=\frac{5}{6} \sigma
\end{aligned}

